Enhancement: Add SlateDB file history and path resolution commands

Vaultic can now answer historical path questions from SlateDB metadata without
falling back to current filesystem pointers or scanning legacy JSON indexes.

The new `vaultic index file-history <path>` command resolves a path through the
immutable directory revisions captured by each snapshot and reports when the path
was created, modified, rebound to a different inode, deleted, or not covered by a
snapshot's backup scope. `--snapshots` annotates changes with the snapshots that
captured them, `--content` distinguishes content changes from metadata-only
changes, and `--inode <fsid>:<inode>` reports the revision history of a specific
inode.

The new `vaultic index path-at <path> --snapshot <id>` command resolves one path
inside one historical snapshot and reports the inode, revision, node type, and
directory revision chain used by the immutable walk.

SlateDB metadata now also stores a commit-ordered snapshot index, `sc:`, so
snapshot history can be scanned in commit order and rebuilt from authoritative
snapshot records by `vaultic index rebuild-pack-stats`. Legacy repositories fail
explicitly for these commands rather than returning answers from current state.

